{"name":"Radioactive","hex":"#89fe05","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"radioactive","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Exhibiting radioactivity.","Dangerous and disgusting, particularly of people or ideas."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any radioactive substance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/radioactive"}],"entries":[{"title":"Radioactive (Imagine Dragons song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Radioactive\" is a song by American pop rock band Imagine Dragons from their major-label debut EP Continued Silence and later on their debut studio album, Night Visions (2012), as the lead single. It was first sent to modern rock radio on October 29, 2012, and then released to contemporary hit radio on April 9, 2013. Musically, \"Radioactive\" is an electronic rock and alternative rock song with elements of dubstep."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/91/Imagine_Dragons_-_%22Radioactive%22_%28Single%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radioactive_(Imagine_Dragons_song)","wikidata":"Q7281218"},{"title":"Radioactive (Yelawolf album)","blocks":[{"text":"Radioactive is the second studio album and major label debut by American rapper Yelawolf. It was released on November 21, 2011 through Ghet-O-Vision Entertainment, DGC Records, Shady Records, and Interscope Records. The album features guest appearances from Shawty Fatt, Mystikal, Kid Rock, Lil Jon, Rittz, Gangsta Boo, Eminem, Poo Bear, Priscilla Renea, Fefe Dobson, Mona Moua and Killer Mike."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/4b/Yelawolf-Radioactive.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radioactive_(Yelawolf_album)","wikidata":"Q1630321"},{"title":"Radioactive decay","blocks":[{"text":"Radioactive decay is the process by which an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by radiation. A material containing unstable nuclei is considered radioactive. Three of the most common types of decay are alpha, beta, and gamma decay. The weak force is the mechanism that is responsible for beta decay, while the other two are governed by the electromagnetic and nuclear forces."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/NuclearReaction.svg/330px-NuclearReaction.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radioactive_decay","wikidata":"Q3332922"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Imagine_Dragons_%40_The_Pageant.jpg/500px-Imagine_Dragons_%40_The_Pageant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Imagine_Dragons_%40_The_Pageant.jpg/500px-Imagine_Dragons_%40_The_Pageant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Imagine Dragons performing \"Radioactive\" at The Pageant in St. Louis, Missouri.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AImagine_Dragons_%40_The_Pageant.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/fd/Imagine_Dragons_and_Kendrick_Lamar_-_%22Radioactive%22_%28Single%29.jpg/250px-Imagine_Dragons_and_Kendrick_Lamar_-_%22Radioactive%22_%28Single%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/fd/Imagine_Dragons_and_Kendrick_Lamar_-_%22Radioactive%22_%28Single%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/fd/Imagine_Dragons_and_Kendrick_Lamar_-_%22Radioactive%22_%28Single%29.jpg/250px-Imagine_Dragons_and_Kendrick_Lamar_-_%22Radioactive%22_%28Single%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AImagine_Dragons_and_Kendrick_Lamar_-_%22Radioactive%22_(Single).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg/500px-Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg/1280px-Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg/500px-Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pierre and Marie Curie in their Paris laboratory, before 1907","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg/500px-Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg/500px-Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Taking an X-ray image with early Crookes tube apparatus in 1896. The Crookes tube is visible in the centre. The standing man is viewing his hand with a fluoroscope screen; this was a common way of setting up the tube. No precautions against radiation exposure are being taken; its hazards were not known at the time.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Radioactivity_and_radiation.png/500px-Radioactivity_and_radiation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Radioactivity_and_radiation.png/500px-Radioactivity_and_radiation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&u